  "rationale": "**Verdict:** the requirement is met. The implementation is marked, and a real-Symposium test covers the scenario. The test is a standalone script that needs `cargo-agents`, so it is easy to skip. I did not find it wired into CI or `cargo test`, and the requirement does not ask for that.\n\n**Implementation.** `Cargo.toml` line 13 carries `{impl}` for `requirements.integration.symposium` on the `[package.metadata.symposium]` block.\n- The block declares the plugin `name = \"habeas\"`.\n- `defaults.skills = false` makes skills opt-in, so the user must enable the plugin. This matches \"let the user enable Habeas's plugin\".\n- `[[package.metadata.symposium.skills]] source.path = \"assets/skills\"` ships the crate's own skills. `assets/skills` contains three skills, so Symposium resolves them from the selected `habeas` crate version. This is what lets skills follow a version change.\n- The `installations` and `hooks` tables in the same metadata belong to `symposium_cli` and `symposium_setup_hook`. They are marked for those requirements, so they are not unmarked behavior for this one.\n\n**Test.** `tests/symposium_hook_smoke.py` has a `{test}` marker for this requirement at line 203. It uses a real `cargo-agents` and a consumer crate that depends on `habeas`:\n- It runs `cargo agents sync` under a pty. It waits for the \"Enable it?\" prompt and answers it. This covers interactive enablement.\n- It checks that the decision to enable `habeas` is saved in the Symposium config.\n- It checks that every skill under `assets/skills` is installed byte-identically into `.agents/skills`.\n- It then swaps in a `habeas` crate with the next patch version and a modified skill, and checks that Cargo selects that version. After a non-interactive `cargo agents sync`, it checks that the installed skill matches the new content. This covers \"after the selected version changes\".\n- It copies the real manifest and only substitutes the CLI installation line with a local runner, so the shipped plugin metadata is what gets exercised.\n\n**Minor observations (not gaps).** The script is run manually with `python3 tests/symposium_hook_smoke.py`. It also depends on Symposium-specific behavior, such as the 5-second debounce, and the script comments say so. The test covers the interesting points of the requirement: enablement, initial skills, and refresh after a version change.",
